# {{ project }} — Agent Notes

## Repository Purpose and structure

{{ project }} defines ecflow suites built on top of [pyflow](https://github.com/ecmwf/pyflow) and
[wellies](https://github.com/ecmwf/pyflow-wellies). Deployment uses
[tracksuite](https://github.com/ecmwf/tracksuite). It was initially created by `wellies quickstart` command.

{{ description | default("") }}
```tree
configs/
  user.yaml          # main configuration, including suite name, ecflow server and other deployment and execution options.
  host.yaml          # execution host configuration / resource manager settings.
  tools.yaml         # installables tools and task runtime context configuration. Installation on the execution host is handled by the suite's `init` family.
  data.yaml          # installables data definitions. Fetching/instantiation of data is handled by the suite's `init` family.
deploy.py            # main python entry point; parses config files and builds the ecFlow suite.
build.sh             # convenience wrapper with different use cases and configuration selections. Main launching point for deployment.
profiles.yaml        # named set of configuration options. Used by `build.sh` to select different deployments.
{{ project }}/
  scripts/           Scripts/snippets used as templates or task script body sources within pyflow.
    hello.sh
  nodes.py           # main `pyflow`-based suite definition, built by `deploy.py`
  config.py          # Configuration data model definition. Built by `deploy.py` from the YAML files in `configs/`
tests/               # suite-level unit tests.
    test_configs.py  # Basic configration and deployment tests. Uses `pytest`.
bin/                 # Executables that will be used in the suite. Treat them either as installables tools.
  hello_world.py
```

## Co-development

If the user asks for your help and you see the state of the project looking like the base template, take your time and ask questions
about how the project should work. Be thoughful about covering as much as possible, including aspects related to:
- deployment environment and dependencies
- ecflow server and execution host configuration
- suite design, structure and purpose
- tools and data installables, and their runtime context
- task scripts and their runtime context

You should assume there is an existing server for you to work with, but always test. If something is missing, stop and ask.

Be mindful of local paths being broadcasted to remote machines, and the opposite. DO NOT TRY to find out remote locations by
yourself, present the issue and ask.

## Vocabulary

- **Suite/Node/Family/Task replace**: means structural changes to the ecflow definition, changes status
and current variable values of already started workflows
- **deploy(ment)**: means regenerating scripts and definition file. Those changes are picked up automatically by
the ecflow_server as soon as deployment is complete. Unless, there are changes to variables or node attribute values.

## How to Deploy

Prioritise the `build.sh` script as the main entry point for deployment. There may be different set-up steps
depending on the machine you are running on, inpect `build.sh` to check compatibility. **STOP AND ASK** when things go wrong.

```shell
# On ECMWF HPC
./build.sh user  # or: module load wellies/new && ./deploy.py configs/config.yaml configs/host.yaml configs/tools_data.yaml
```

Whilst running, `tracksuite` will show a diff-like list of modified files. It will also ask for confirmation (input `y`).

If users requests a summary of changes, do your own diff in the same directories that the `tracksuite` CLI is
doing, inspects the changes across all files and summarise. Some changes can be broadcasted to many files (like changing
the ecflow version will change every single script), but those can be summarised as one change. YOU MUST DO YOUR DIFF **BEFORE**
accepting tracksuite's input, otherwise deployment will go forward and staging/local will be synced. Start a new terminal if needed.

USE TRACKSUITE'S diff when you want to double-check your changes in scripts are as expected or to confirm some behaviour in script generation.

## Configuration

Configuration is constructed by stacking multiple YAML files in `configs/` directory.
The `Config` class in `deploy.py` merges these via `wellies.parse_yaml_files`.
Duplicated entries are overridden by the last file in the stack, so the order matters, except
for the block `ecflow_variables` which is merged across all files.

Values can be overriden at runtime by passing them to `deploy.py` as CLI arguments, for example:
```shell
./build.sh user -s suite_name=my_new_suite
```

This can only **change** existing keys, not create new ones. Use this option for runtime or very contextual
changes to the base configuration. If such changes are expect to linger or be version controlled, create new config files. In doing so,
avoid defining the same components (like the ecflow_server definition) multiple times.

`wellies` provides a simple templating system for configuration entries,
so values can reference other values in the configuration. For example:

```yaml
suite_name: my_suite
suite_dir: /path/to/suites/\{suite_name\}
```

It also provides some global template variables like `{ HOME }` or `{ USER }`. **ATTENTION**: This will always resolve to
local values during deployment, which may be invalid if the project is deployed remotely from the ecflow-server or execution host.
Always check the resolved values in the deployment summary and ask if you are not sure.

## Split of Contexts

There are three different contexts that a suite will interact with:
- **Deployment**: the environment where `deploy.py` is run, which needs to have access to the repository and its
files, and all the dependencies required by `deploy.py` and `wellies` for deployment. Deployment may include sending generated files to
a remote machine.
- **ecflow-server**: the environment where the ecflow-server is running, which needs to have access to the generated suite
definition file and all the files referenced by it, including scripts, headers and manual files. This may be a local machine,
a remote machine in a shared or isolated filesystem. The deployment tools are able to take care of it, provided the correct configuration is set.
- **Task execution**: the environment where the tasks are executed, which needs to have access to job files, executables and data.
The ecflow-server is responsible to communicate with the execution host. Some level of configuration is available to specify what, where and how
this communication happens and ensure certain tools are available at runtime, via the `tools.load("mytool")` script.

**IMPORTANT**: DO NOT MIX THESE CONTEXTS OR ASSUME HAVING DIRECT ACCESS TO DEPLOYED FILES IS A VALID SHORTCUT.

### Deployment context

Always inspect in the `build.sh` script the current accepted deployment setup.
If you require to build a local copy, be sure you are using the same versions.

- `ecflow` (HPC module or conda package from `conda-forge`)
- `wellies` (HPC module or pip installable from indexed package `pyflow-wellies`)
- `pyflow` (comes with `wellies` HPC module or pip installable from indexed package `pyflow-workflow-generator`)
- `tracksuite` (comes with `wellies` HPC module or pip installable from indexed package `tracksuite`)

> IMPORTANT: **NEVER** use `defaults` channel for conda packages, **only** `conda-forge`. If you can't find a package in conda-forge, stop.

### ecflow-server context

The `ecflow_server` configuration block provides all options you may want to check or change regarding the ecflow-server context.
The most important ones are:

- **hostname**: the machine where the server is running.
- *deploy_dir*: The target directory, in **hostname**, where generated files are sent by deployment.
- **user**: the user that runs the server and executes the deployment, which needs to have access to all generated files and target directories on *host*.

### Task runtime context

Each node can specify a execution host and runtime context configuration. For the host, it is defined in `host.yaml` and the most
important options are:

- **hostname**: the machine where the task is executed. This is directly associated with the underlying `pyflow.Host` class.
- **user**: the user that executes the task.
- **log_directory**: the directory where task job outputs are stored.
- **submit_arguments**: the arguments passed to the underlying `pyflow.Host` submit method,
  which is responsible to send the task to the execution host. This is where you can specify resource manager
  options, for example, the number of cores, memory or walltime.

This sets how a job will be submitted and the queing system config level. For internal tasks runtime context, you need
to work with `wellies`'s tools configuration. In the task script you can then use `config.tools.load("tool_name")` to
create a loading snippet for that tool. Tools can be grouped in environments and have a dependency tree. Check wellies documentation
for more examples.

## Development environment

If building a local development environment or running outside of ECMWF's HPC system, create a minimal conda environment with `ecflow`

```shell
mamba create -c conda-forge python=3.11 ecflow -n {{ project }}-env
```

then install other packages using `pip`.

> Note: If using conda on ECMWF's HPC system, you must first load it module with: `module load conda`. This disables any other module currently loaded and the ability to load any other module other than conda.

## More notes for Agents

- For more details about any of the comments below, check the appropriate documentation or source code. All are open-source projects.
- `ecflow` is a client-server system, but also a scripting language for job-like tasks.
- `pyflow` provides a more Pythonic way to define suites and tasks, and acts as a compiler that generates the `ecflow` scripts and suite definition file.
- `wellies` provides a configuration framework and interface to `tracksuite` deployment tool.
- Treat deployed/generated files as READ-ONLY. Always go through the deployment process to change them.
- ALWAYS go through the ecflow_client commands to interact with the server, run tasks or change nodes.
- `deploy.py` uses `tracksuite` under the hood (via wellies) for git-tracked suite deployment with `git commit` on the deploy directory.
- When running any command that requires a task runtime context or environment, inspect the task jobfile to find out how to reproduce such environment. You
  can ignore everything that is related to `ecflow` like exporting `EC_*`
  variables or loading the `ecflow` module.
- `pyflow` enforces certain structure for generated scripts, specially with variables. At deploy time, the in-memory
  script body is inspected for used bash variables, all that are also ecflow variables are automatically exported in the script header.
  This means that if you want to use a variable in the script body, you need to make sure it is defined as an ecflow variable.
- USE `ecflow_client requeue --aborted` whenever possible, this requeues all aborted tasks under a node.
- A node with a repeat will be requeued as a whole as soon as all nodes in it are complete. Without guardrails, cycles runs freely.
  You can still access older logs even if the node is queued, just make sure the logs you are reading are the ones you want to read.
- When you `execute` or `rerun` a node, the ecflow_server increments the ECF_TRYNO variable. This create new log files and can be use to compare different runs.
  When a task is requeued, the ECF_TRYNO is reset to 0, until it is submitted again.
- If you replace a node with a `Repeat` attribute, this will reset the cycle to the start. Replacing children nodes under it, does NOT have the same effect.
  If restarting the cycle is not the intent and you still want to replace the whole node, follow:
  1. suspend
  2. get current step value
  3. replace
  4. change repeat variable value to original value
  5. resume.

